  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Tiapride may interact with Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase through multiple indirect pathways. The drug is directly linked to 6-phosphofructo-2-kinase/fructose-2,6-bisphosphatase 4, which in turn is targeted by Isograndifoliol and 4-Methyl-5-[(2Z)-2-{[4-(4-morpholinyl)phenyl]imino}-2,5-dihydro-4-pyrimidinyl]-1,3-thiazol-2-amine. These compounds are all targets of Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, indicating a potential downstream effect of Tiapride on this enzyme. Additionally, Tiapride is linked to Surface protein A, which is targeted by 5-amino-1-(4-chlorophenyl)-1H-pyrazole-4-carbonitrile, another compound that targets Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. While the direct interaction between Tiapride and Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase is not explicitly stated, the evidence suggests a plausible mechanistic pathway through these intermediaries. The integration of multiple targets and their shared downstream effect on Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase supports a potential role for Tiapride in modulating this enzyme, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction.",
